00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
hello and welcome to the cheeky science  show where in this video i'm going to  talk about a recent study that came out  of davidson claire's lab  whereby they developed two different  mouse frailty clocks  one which can predict a mouse's  biological age  and a second clock that can predict when  it will die this not only sets the  stones for further developing a clock  that can work in humans  but also provides a useful tool that can  be applied by  other scientists trying to understand  different interventions  of aging and health span in mice so i'll  begin by addressing what actually is  aging  and the current methods for determining  biological age  and then we'll discuss the highlights  from this recent nature paper  and then finally i'll give some thoughts  and opinions so firstly then  what is aging well one way to define  aging is the progressive deterioration  of an organism over time  and so it's thought that aging is a  dynamic non-linear process  and what that means is basically each of  us age at different rates  in different ways but the problem of  this is that it makes studying aging  really challenging  and so there are two ways that you can  define age chronological age  and biological age so chronological age  refers to the amount of time that has  passed since you were born  whilst biological age is a  time-dependent component of an  organism's overall health  and regarding studies of aging  biological age is being increasingly  used because it aims to more accurately  reflect  aging in an individual than the  conventional chronological age  mobile's chronological age can be easily  determined if you know  when an individual was born there are  various different ways and approaches  that are being developed to try and  determine biological age  i recently highlighted these different  biological aging clocks in a video  that include different clocks such as an  epigenetic clock based on dna  methylation  a proteomic agent clock and clock space  and telomere length and gene expression  all of these different clocks are united  by the fact that they aim to try and  find a way to quantify and define aging  and to be able to evaluate the efficacy  of different aging interventions  however some of the approaches that i've  just mentioned such as dna methylation  clocks  have the caveats of being currently  quite expensive  time consuming and requiring the  extraction of blood or tissue  from an individual and for these reasons  some labs may not have access  to be able to perform some of these  clocks  to be able to understand biological age  in different aging  interventions so one goal would be to  find  an approach that can be more  non-invasive  cheaper and can be employed by a greater  number of people  i'd say that's what they sought to  explore in this recent paper as i  mentioned from davidson clare's lab  and this is from the article age and  life expectancy clocks  based on machine learning analysis of  mouse frailty  and so given by the title they are using  mouse frailty measures  in combination with machine learning  approaches to come up with two different  clocks  one that can determine a mouse's  biological age and a second clock  that can predict how long the mice has  left to live  so to develop both of these clocks the  team needed data  of mice both to originally develop and  then subsequently to test the efficacy  of these clocks  so to get the data they tracked frailty  in a cohort of  aging mice from 21 months of age until  their natural deaths  and so this was in male mice and by  frailty what i mean is they took  data that recorded the frailty index and  they did this  approximately every six weeks in their  population of aging male mice  until the end of their lives so for my  attentive viewers  you may recall that i mentioned the  frailty index when i mentioned  alpha-ketoglutarate in a previous video  but to recap the frailty index  is an assessment that includes and  quantifies  the accumulation of up to 70 different  health-related deficits  including laboratory test results  symptoms diseases  and standard measures such as activities  of daily living  and so it may include factors such as  hearing loss and gait disorders  and so once these different health  related deficits are assessed  you end up with a number between 0 and 1  with the higher the number indicating a  greater degree of frailty  so as we saw with the  alpha-ketoglutarate study  frailty index can be used as an  indicator of how effective different  interventions  are in terms of improving health span  however the authors of this paper wanted  to take  it a bit further and explore how frailty  index could actually be used to model  biological age or predict life  expectancy  so i said at the beginning aging is  dynamic and non-linear  and this is most evident in this figure  here where you can see  the different frailty index measures of  each of the different mice that they  assess in the study  and so we can see firstly that there is  a general  increase of the frailty index which is  expected  however you can see that there's also  much variability within the population  so the frailty index takes into account  each of the different  health-related deficits equally however  the authors suspected that a potentially  more predictive model of biological age  could be achieved  if the different metrics were  differentially weighted within the model  and so by doing this it can take into  account how  individual frailty index items vary in  their correlation with age  and that's most evidence in the second  figure here where you can see that  tail stiffening breathing rate and gait  disorders  hearing loss kyphosis and tremor have  pretty strong correlations  with chronological age conversely some  of the items had no correlation  this included fission loss loss of  whiskers and  cornual capacity just to name a couple  and so the authors took this information  and tried a variety of different  mathematical models to see what would  work best  and eventually they decided that the  random forest models  was most effective and they terms the  outcome clock of their model  fright age for frailty inferred  geriatric health timeline  so the key token is that fright age is  superior to frailty index  at being able to predict chronological  age however fright age wasn't so good  at being able to predict mortality so  to develop a clock that can predict  lifespan the authors  decided to look at the correlation  between each of these  individual frailty index items and  survival and their essential outcome  was the afraid clock standing for  analysis of frailty and death  and so clearly the lab are continuing  the trend of naming these different  clocks such because aging and death are  frightening to many people  and so it goes along with grim age clock  that  is used to look at dna methylation  patterns in humans  and is named after the grim reaper as  the clock  allows one to predict lifespan and  health span and so the nice thing about  the afraid clock is as it has the  potential  to compare lifespan effects of different  interventions  before the death of the different mice  so do these clocks  actually work and will they be effective  so to test their clocks they first use  previously published data  that had treated mice with enalapril  which improved health span but not  lifespan  and they found that their their model  validated their results  as you can see here that an alloprol  in the fright age showed a decrease but  the afraid score was  the same and showed no significant  differences indicative  of being no extension of lifespan  conversely when they placed mice on  methionine restriction  which has been previously shown to  extend both healthspan and lifespan of  male mice  and so by calculating the fright age  they found that the  methionine restricted mice had an age  0.7 months younger than the control fed  mice  and by using their afraid clock they  could predict  that the methionine restricted mice  would live 1.3 months longer  than controls and so they concluded that  their fright age and afraid clock models  are responsive to health span and  lifespan extending interventions  and so the real advantage of using these  different clocks  and using the frailty index measurements  is that  frailty index is really quick and pretty  much free to assess  and doesn't really require any  specialized equipment or training  and it also doesn't have any impacts to  the animals and so the authors  definitely encourage  longevity studies to try and incorporate  periodic frailty assessments  and then to use their clocks to have a  better measurement  of the chronological age and to be able  to predict how long the mice has until  it dies  and so the clocks don't make other  measurements of biological age redundant  it instead provides a potential tool  that will enable  a more accurate comparison between  different intervention studies  so we can have a better understanding of  what these different interventions are  impacting  on health span and lifespan and to  better understand the aging process  itself  now there are some limitations currently  firstly that they  developed these models using  measurements on male mice  and so it'll be very important to also  do a similar analysis on female mice  because there definitely are sex  dependent differences  in aging and longevity moreover there  are limitations  in using these complex mathematical  models including a lack of  interpretability of the waiting and  interactions of the variables  nonetheless the models can be further  improved by including a larger number of  mice to begin with  and also including both more mice  especially at the old ages  but also assessing felty from ages  younger than 21 months of age which is  when they started the study on the mice  and so due to the fact that a lot of  data is needed to develop  such models it may take a while to be  able to apply the same  approaches to develop a clock for human  frailty index data  but in theory the afraid clock could be  easily adapted  to predict mortality from human frailty  index data  but this research highlighted in this  paper is definitely a good step forward  to achieving this goal  so i hope this video has given you some  insight into this recent publication  and i hope you've learned something and  as always thanks for listening

